# tmux-share.sysusers: accounts for sysusers.d(5).
#
# Install as /etc/sysusers.d/tmux-share.conf (or /usr/lib/sysusers.d/).
#
# This file declares exactly one thing: the group `tmux-share`. That group is
# the reader group. The tmpfiles rules (tmux-share.tmpfiles) give it
# traverse-only access to the runtime directory and read-only access to the
# transcript, and nothing else.
#
# THE READER ACCOUNT IS NOT CREATED HERE. Which account reads the transcript is
# a deployment choice: the operator names an account that already exists on
# their system, and this file must not assume or invent one. To grant access,
# the operator runs, once, as root:
#
#     usermod -aG tmux-share <reader>
#
# (The reader must start a new login session for the membership to apply.)
# To revoke access: `gpasswd -d <reader> tmux-share`.
#
# NO SERVICE ACCOUNT IS DECLARED, deliberately. Every tmux-share component (the
# tmux server, the transcript tap, the watchdog, the trimmer) runs as one
# account: root by default, or the existing account that `install.sh --user`
# names. The transcript is a record of what that account's shell does and the
# runtime files belong to it. This file never creates that account, and it
# never needs to: a dedicated service user would only matter if components ran
# under different accounts, and none does. Declaring an unused user would add
# an account to the host for no benefit.
#
# Type  Name        ID
# "-" lets the system pick a free GID (a system-range GID, as for any
# sysusers group); nothing depends on a fixed number.
g       tmux-share  -
